---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base title="Distributed merge semantics · enprot">
  <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold mb-3">Distributed merge semantics</h1>
  <p class="text-enprot-muted mb-6">
    How enprot files merge lock-free in git and other DVCS systems.
  </p>

  <section class="mb-10">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-3">The key insight</h2>
    <p class="mb-3">
      Enprot mutations are already <em>content-addressable</em> (CAS) and <em>capability-scoped</em>
      (per-WORD). They <strong>commute at the block level</strong>: two parties editing different
      WORDs produce disjoint changes that merge trivially; two parties editing the same WORD with
      the same password and deterministic AEAD produce identical ciphertext — also a no-op merge.
    </p>
    <p>
      That's exactly the property git exploits internally, and it means enprot can be merge-friendly
      without locks if we lean into it deliberately.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-10">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-3">Five principles</h2>

    <h3 class="font-semibold mt-4 mb-1">1. Deterministic AEAD is the default</h3>
    <p class="mb-3 text-sm">
      Random-IV encryption breaks content-addressing. Two parties encrypting the same plaintext
      independently get different ciphertexts, forcing a conflict. With det variants (already
      shipped as <code>-det</code>), they get identical ciphertext and the merge is a no-op.
    </p>

    <h3 class="font-semibold mt-4 mb-1">2. Every encrypted block is CAS-referenced</h3>
    <p class="mb-3 text-sm">
      Inline <code>DATA</code> lines put ciphertext in the file text → two candidate ciphertexts
      become textual conflicts. CAS-referenced means the file holds a hash pointer; the candidates
      coexist in CAS as different blobs. Merge decision becomes "which hash?" — much smaller
      surface, and "both" is always an option (additive merge).
    </p>
    <p class="mb-3 text-sm text-enprot-muted">
      This is git's blob/tree split applied to enprot: ciphertext = blob, WORD region = tree node,
      file = commit-ish.
    </p>

    <h3 class="font-semibold mt-4 mb-1">3. Chain anchors are DAG nodes, not linear links</h3>
    <p class="mb-3 text-sm">
      Linear "block N+1 references N" doesn't merge. Git-commit-shaped anchors with multiple
      parents do. See <a href="/docs/chain-dag" class="text-enprot-accent hover:underline">Chain DAG</a>.
    </p>

    <h3 class="font-semibold mt-4 mb-1">4. Capability sets accumulate, they don't collide</h3>
    <p class="mb-3 text-sm">
      Each block carries its capability requirement. When Alice and Bob merge, the merged file's
      capability set is the <strong>union</strong> of theirs. Identity-based access would have to
      pick a winner; capability-based never does. Even at a genuine same-WORD conflict, the
      additive resolution keeps both blocks — the merged file's capability set still grows.
    </p>

    <h3 class="font-semibold mt-4 mb-1">5. Conflicts are reported in capability terms</h3>
    <p class="mb-3 text-sm">
      A conflict between two <code>ENCRYPTED X</code> blocks isn't a textual conflict — it's
      "Decryptor-X-with-password-A vs. Decryptor-X-with-password-B". The merge driver surfaces
      that framing:
    </p>
    <pre class="bg-enprot-ink text-enprot-paper p-4 rounded overflow-x-auto text-sm"><code>// &lt;( CONFLICT Agent_007 )&gt;
// &lt;( OURS )&gt;     capability: Decryptor-Agent_007, key-fp 0xABCD…
... block …
// &lt;( THEIRS )&gt;   capability: Decryptor-Agent_007, key-fp 0x1234…
... block …
// &lt;( END CONFLICT )&gt;</code></pre>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-10">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-3">The merge algorithm</h2>
    <p class="mb-3">A 3-way merge (base, ours, theirs) at the WORD-region level:</p>
    <table class="w-full border border-enprot-ink/10 text-sm">
      <thead class="bg-enprot-ink/5">
        <tr><th class="p-2 text-left">Case</th><th class="p-2 text-left">Result</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="p-2">Different WORDs touched</td><td class="p-2">Auto-merge; no conflict</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="p-2">Same WORD, same content (det AEAD matched)</td><td class="p-2">Auto-merge; identical hashes</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="p-2">Same WORD, different content</td><td class="p-2">Conflict marker; offer additive resolution</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="p-2">One side adds new WORD region</td><td class="p-2">Auto-merge; canonical position (alphabetical)</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="p-2">One side deletes, other modifies</td><td class="p-2">Conflict (irreducible)</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="p-2">Both sides add chain anchors off same parent</td><td class="p-2">DAG node with two parents (no conflict)</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="p-2">Both sides add chain anchors off different parents</td><td class="p-2">DAG node with both as parents (merge commit)</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm">
      The driver lives in <code>.gitattributes</code> as <code>*.ept merge=enprot diff=enprot</code>,
      pointing at <code>enprot merge-driver %O %A %B %L %P</code> (standard git merge-driver
      interface). Plain-text regions fall back to git's normal diff3.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-10">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-3">Real-life workflow</h2>
    <pre class="bg-enprot-ink text-enprot-paper p-4 rounded overflow-x-auto text-sm"><code># Alice and Bob both work on contracts.ept
git clone &lt;repo&gt;
cd repo/

# Alice encrypts a new clause
enprot encrypt --cipher aes-256-gcm-siv-det -w NewClause \
    -k NewClause=alicepassword contracts.ept
git add contracts.ept
git commit -m "Add NewClause"
git push

# Bob concurrently encrypts a different clause
enprot encrypt --cipher aes-256-gcm-siv-det -w OtherClause \
    -k OtherClause=bobpassword contracts.ept
git add contracts.ept
git commit -m "Add OtherClause"
git pull --rebase  # ← merge-driver handles NewClause vs OtherClause cleanly
git push</code></pre>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm">
      No locking. No coordination. Both clauses end up in the merged file, each with its own
      capability requirement.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-3">What this doesn't solve</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-1">
      <li><strong>Structural conflicts</strong> — renesting, reordering children of a WORD region. No algorithm auto-merges; human resolution required.</li>
      <li><strong>CAS divergence</strong> — if Alice's repo doesn't have the blob Bob's hash references, she can't decrypt that block until she gets the blob. CAS sync is <code>rsync</code>/git-lfs territory, not enprot.</li>
      <li><strong>Password rotation</strong> — if Alice rotates WORD X's password, Bob's old Decryptor-X capability no longer opens the new ciphertext. That's a <em>key distribution</em> problem (telling Bob the new password), out of scope.</li>
      <li><strong>Replay of old signed anchors</strong> — DAG verification must check parent-before-child hash ordering <em>and</em> monotonic timestamps if present.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</Base>
